WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.954
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.954 --> 00:00:02.385
Monday the sixteenth.

00:00:02.385 --> 00:00:08.656
A cold, bright start to the week, with a plain workday on the calendar and nothing after it.

00:00:08.656 --> 00:00:11.177
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:16.902
That long random link on the memo feed can't be guessed, so how does it leak anyway?

00:00:16.902 --> 00:00:18.265
Answer's at the end.

00:00:18.265 --> 00:00:19.765
Weather's dry all day.

00:00:19.765 --> 00:00:24.876
It starts in the upper thirties and gets to about fifty by early afternoon.

00:00:24.876 --> 00:00:31.828
The wind's the thing again, northwest in the mid-teens with gusts near thirty from about noon to four.

00:00:31.828 --> 00:00:32.782
Okay, the day.

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:35.849
Calendar has the gym at seven, straight sets.

00:00:35.849 --> 00:00:37.757
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:37.757 --> 00:00:41.233
Nothing on there after that, so the evening's open.

00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:42.187
Alright, news.

00:00:42.187 --> 00:00:43.619
One from your corner.

00:00:43.619 --> 00:00:47.708
Google and Samsung opened preorders on their Gemini glasses.

00:00:47.708 --> 00:00:53.705
They start at about four hundred dollars in Warby Parker frames and ship December tenth.

00:00:53.705 --> 00:00:55.886
There's no screen in the lenses.

00:00:55.886 --> 00:01:03.315
It's a twelve megapixel camera, speakers, and a voice assistant you can ask about whatever you're looking at.

00:01:03.315 --> 00:01:06.041
The privacy part is the interesting bit.

00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:13.811
The camera won't work if you cover its recording light, and faces of people around you get blurred before the A.I.

00:01:13.811 --> 00:01:14.969
sees the picture.

00:01:14.969 --> 00:01:19.195
A camera on your face all day is a strange new kind of camera.

00:01:19.195 --> 00:01:22.126
Closer to home, a follow-up on food stamps.

00:01:22.126 --> 00:01:31.326
Mayor Mamdani says he worked out a deal with Trump to push back a new food stamp rule that small shops like bodegas were worried about.

00:01:31.326 --> 00:01:36.506
It's a delay of about ninety days, and it applies nationwide, not just here.

00:01:36.506 --> 00:01:40.936
It's a pause, not a fix, so the fight comes back in the new year.

00:01:40.936 --> 00:01:44.684
And the Nvidia story from yesterday has a next chapter.

00:01:44.684 --> 00:01:49.455
The company reports earnings tomorrow, its first without Jensen Huang.

00:01:49.455 --> 00:01:56.407
And three House committee chairs have asked for a closed-door briefing on Wednesday about why he left.

00:01:56.407 --> 00:02:00.837
So there's a real chance we hear more about the reason this week.

00:02:00.837 --> 00:02:03.359
Okay, here's one for later this week.

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:09.288
A new ramen shop called Men-Ya Inoichi just opened on Knickerbocker Avenue in Bushwick.

00:02:09.288 --> 00:02:15.354
It's got nine different noodle bowls, including a tonkotsu with pork belly and a dan dan.

00:02:15.354 --> 00:02:20.329
And it does izakaya stuff on the side, like grilled skewers and takoyaki.

00:02:20.329 --> 00:02:25.713
A hot bowl after a cold windy ride is about as good a plan as a Wednesday gets.

00:02:25.713 --> 00:02:28.099
Today's challenge is for your body.

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:34.164
Tonight, sit down on the floor cross-legged, then stand back up without using your hands.

00:02:34.164 --> 00:02:35.459
Try it three times.

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:37.299
If it's easy, do it slower.

00:02:37.299 --> 00:02:40.707
If you need a hand, notice which side you lean on.

00:02:40.707 --> 00:02:49.772
It's a quick check on hips, legs, and balance all at once, and it's one of those things that quietly gets harder if you never try it.

00:02:49.772 --> 00:02:52.293
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:02:52.293 --> 00:02:56.042
Remember picking the memo's voice off that leaderboard?

00:02:56.042 --> 00:03:03.334
Here's what the numbers next to each voice actually mean, and why a gap of ten points is basically nothing.

00:03:03.334 --> 00:03:05.788
An Elo score is really a prediction.

00:03:05.788 --> 00:03:10.763
The gap between two scores tells you how often one should beat the other.

00:03:10.763 --> 00:03:16.692
A gap of a hundred points means the higher one should win about two times out of three.

00:03:16.692 --> 00:03:20.713
A gap of four hundred means it should win about ten to one.

00:03:20.713 --> 00:03:24.189
And two voices with the same score are a coin flip.

00:03:24.189 --> 00:03:26.098
Each vote nudges the scores.

00:03:26.098 --> 00:03:31.345
If the favorite wins, it barely moves, because that's what the math expected.

00:03:31.345 --> 00:03:35.775
If the underdog wins, it jumps, because the prediction was wrong.

00:03:35.775 --> 00:03:37.411
That's the whole engine.

00:03:37.411 --> 00:03:40.819
Surprises move scores, and expected results don't.

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:41.977
Here's the catch.

00:03:41.977 --> 00:03:46.544
With classic Elo, the order of the votes changes the final numbers.

00:03:46.544 --> 00:03:49.747
So most arenas don't run it one vote at a time.

00:03:49.747 --> 00:03:56.631
They take every vote at once and solve for the set of scores that best explains all of them together.

00:03:56.631 --> 00:03:58.471
Same idea, steadier answer.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.583
And then there's the error bar.

00:04:00.583 --> 00:04:06.785
A voice with a few hundred votes might sit at a score give or take twenty or thirty points.

00:04:06.785 --> 00:04:12.715
So if the top two are ten points apart, the leaderboard can't actually tell them apart.

00:04:12.715 --> 00:04:15.509
That's why some arenas give shared ranks.

00:04:15.509 --> 00:04:23.142
When you picked a voice near the top, you were really picking from a tie, and your own ears were the tiebreaker.

00:04:23.142 --> 00:04:28.118
So: calendar says gym at seven and Godinger till five, then a free night.

00:04:28.118 --> 00:04:29.890
Try the no-hands stand-up.

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:36.296
The answer: things like chat app previews and browser history carry it places you didn't mean.

00:04:36.296 --> 00:04:38.000
Have a good Monday, Ziad.
